), and W6 appears again in hand. Discarding it breaks no structure, but having two W6s suggests possible overexposure. W6 is not a terminal or honor; low value. Unshown W6: 2 \u2014 not rare.\n- **Play W2**: W2 is a character tile, not involved in any current meld. Others have played W1, W2, W3, W4, W5, W7, W8, W9 \u2014 almost all W tiles are out. W2 may be dangerous if someone is collecting lower characters. But unshown W2: 2 \u2014 still some left.\n- **Play F1**: This discards one from a pair of East Winds. Since F1 is my seat wind, keeping the pair offers at least 2 points (Seat Wind). Discarding one keeps a single F1 \u2014 weak. But if opponent needs F1 to win, this could feed them. However, F4 is already discarded by multiple players (unshown F4: 0), suggesting winds are being used.\n\nNotably, I already have **three kongs/pungs** (B7, B8, and potentially F1 if I keep the pair and form a pung later). If I can promote another kong or complete a fourth pung, **Three Kongs (32 points)** becomes possible. Also, **All Pungs (6 points)** is viable if I move toward pung-based hand.\n\nBut I\u2019m close to completing a high-scoring structure. Another observation: I have two kongs in B7 and B8 \u2014 consecutive number pungs in same suit. With B6 in hand, this hints at **Pure Shifted Pungs** (W23? No \u2014 W6/W7/W8 chow is mixed). Not applicable. But **Three Kongs** is solid.\n\nHowever, my hand is nearly fixed \u2014 I just need to discard safely.\n\nBest defensive and strategic goal: avoid feeding winners while preserving scoring potential.\n\nReason:\n- **F1 pair is worth keeping** for Seat Wind (2 points), and possibly building into a pung if I draw another (but unlikely \u2014 better to preserve than hope).\n- **B6 is the safest discard**: it's not part of any existing meld, not a wind/dragon, and not involved in Seat/Prevailing wind. B6 cannot form a chow easily now (B5 and B7 are nearly gone), and others have strong dot sequences already \u2014 less likely to need B6 specifically.\n- W2 and W6 are more dangerous: W2 could complete 1-2-3 chows for someone in characters, and W6 was part of a visible chow \u2014 opponents may track it.\n- F1 discard risks giving an opponent a winning tile, especially if they are waiting on winds.\n\nThus, **discarding B6** minimizes risk and maintains my pair of F1 for scoring.\n\nAnswer: Play B6"}
